        {
          "headline": "FTC issues 6(b) orders to nine companies over person-to-person pricing and algorithmic pay",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "FTC", "url": "https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2026/10/ftc-launches-personalized-pricing-compensation-inquiry" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "The FTC said on 28 October it issued orders under Section 6(b) of the FTC Act to nine companies: Delta, United, JetBlue, Lyft, Uber, DoorDash, Instacart, Target and Walmart. The orders seek information on whether pricing \"varies from one person to the next based on factors such as participation in loyalty programs\", and documentation of the inputs, tools and procedures used to set compensation and pricing.",
            "The release's term for the systems under scrutiny is \"superintelligence algorithms\"; it does not use the phrase \"artificial intelligence\". Companies have 45 days from receipt to respond.",
            "Chairman Andrew N. Ferguson said \"we intend to scrutinize the opaque methodology that some companies in these industries may be using\", that \"consumers deserve to know whether companies use their web surfing habits or other personal data\", and that \"workers also deserve to know if their compensation is affected by how companies use their personal data\". The vote was 2-0.",
            "A 6(b) study is a compulsory information-gathering exercise, not an enforcement action, and the release names no alleged violation and no timetable for any report."
          ],
          "topics": ["us-federal-policy", "labor", "privacy"],
          "impact": "neutral",
          "flags": ["single-source"]
        },
        {
          "headline": "Copyright Office DMCA notice intends to renew the literary-works text-and-data-mining exemption over publisher objection",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "Federal Register", "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/full_text/text/2026/10/28/2026-21939.txt" }
