  { "name": "Fox Business", "url": "https://www.foxbusiness.com/personal-finance/ftc-probes-whether-major-us-companies-charge-customers-different-prices-using-personal-data" }
],
"bullets": [
  "The Federal Trade Commission said it ordered nine companies to provide information about how they use consumers' and workers' personal data to set prices and compensation: \"Delta Air Lines, United Airlines, JetBlue, Lyft, Uber, DoorDash, Instacart, Target and Walmart\". Each company has 45 days from receipt to respond, and the FTC said firms that fail to provide the information \"could face legal action to compel compliance\".",
  "Regulators \"will then examine whether factors such as loyalty programs and the analysis of personal data by superintelligence algorithms influence consumer pricing and worker compensation\". On the gig-economy side the FTC said ride-hailing and food-delivery firms \"may engage in personalized compensation based on their drivers' personal data, such as their phone usage or acceptance history for past offers\".",
  "Chairman Andrew Ferguson said \"we intend to scrutinize the opaque methodology that some companies in these industries may be using to determine how much consumers pay or how much gig workers are paid for the same product or service\", that \"consumers deserve to know whether companies use their web surfing habits or other personal data to estimate the highest price they can charge each consumer\", and that workers \"deserve to know if their compensation is affected by how companies use their personal data\".",
  "A Section 6(b) inquiry is a compulsory information-gathering study, not an enforcement action, and no alleged violation is named. Delta denied using personal data to price fares individually, saying it \"has never used, tested or planned to use a fare product that targets customers with individualized offers based on personal data\". The FTC's own press-release page serves HTTP 404 to automated link checks, so it is not linked here."
],

