      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "PROBE benchmark: frontier coding agents reach under 21% recall and under 9% precision on 2,457 real bugs",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "A benchmark called PROBE, posted on arXiv, takes every bug fixed over 18 months across 20 GitHub repositories. That's 2,457 bugs. Then it asks whether frontier coding agents find them." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "They all achieve a recall of below 21% and a precision of less than 9%. Stronger models increase recall from 8% to 17%, but they do not improve precision." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The twist is in the title: agents find different bugs than maintainers fix. The paper says LLM and human evaluators still judge 85% of findings valid, so low precision here isn't the same as a worthless finding." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "This is a preprint, not peer reviewed, and a single source." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "Berkeley multi-agent system writes a CUDA kernel 1.2x faster than production FlashInfer MLA paged decode",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "A second arXiv paper goes the other way. A team at the University of California, Berkeley reports a multi-agent system that writes a GPU kernel from scratch and reaches 1.2x mean speedup over the production FlashInfer MLA paged decode kernel." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Over a kernel humans hand-tuned?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Yes. It wins on 36 of 47 production workloads from the FlashInfer benchmark." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "That result needed an extended token budget. It's a preprint, not peer reviewed, and a single source." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "Now the security beat, where today there's a claim and a counterweight." }
      ]
