      "section": "Security, misuse & threat intelligence",
      "headline": "ESET: APT groups use AI to speed up labour-intensive work but it has not reshaped the threat landscape",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "Here's the counterweight. ESET published six months of state-actor telemetry yesterday, covering April to September. It says threat actors used AI primarily to accelerate tasks that would otherwise require painstaking effort." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Not autonomous operations?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "No. ESET says these groups are using AI much like mainstream software developers, and that it has not noticed AI substantially reshaping the APT landscape over the past six months. It did find phishing lures with images that were clearly AI-generated." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "There's one case that stands out. ESET says a Russia-aligned group inserted a decoy request for instructions on building a nuclear weapon into a script comment, apparently to trip the safety guardrails of LLM-based security analysis and stop the malicious code that followed from being inspected." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "That's an attack on the defender's tooling, not the target. This is one vendor's own company claim from its own telemetry, a single source, and the report gives no counts for how many groups or operations involved AI." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "One item from the defence beat." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Military, defense & geopolitics",
      "headline": "Transcom officials say commercial camera networks plus frontier AI expose US force movements",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "US Transportation Command officials, speaking anonymously at a conference in Grapevine, Texas, said commercial cameras, sensors and tracking tools now make it easier to track service members' patterns of life, and to infer force movements, training schedules and mobilisation plans." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And AI is the multiplier?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "One official said frontier models and advanced AI help bad actors exploit those data vulnerabilities at a larger scale than before. Another put it this way: I'm really not as concerned about, can you see my tag. The concern is whether someone can link a plate to intent and timing." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The officials are unnamed, DefenseScoop is a single source, and the piece reports no measured incident." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "To health, and two studies that put numbers on what these systems do." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Health, science & medicine",
      "headline": "Agentic LLM system cut immune-adverse-event chart annotation time by 2.71 minutes in a randomised crossover",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "Nature Cancer published a study today on an agentic system that pulls immune-related adverse events out of clinical notes. In a randomised crossover study of clinical trial staff, the assistance reduced annotation time by 2.71 minutes." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Did accuracy hold up?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "It improved, though only marginally on significance. Complete-match accuracy went up, with an odds ratio of 1.45, a 95% confidence interval of 1.01 to 2.08, and a P value of 0.045. Agreement between annotators improved more clearly. Nature Cancer says the best configuration cost approximately $0.02 per note." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Then the gap between lab and clinic. Retrospectively the system reached a macro-averaged F1 of 0.92 for detecting current events. In prospective silent deployment over 3 months, that fell to between 0.72 and 0.79." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And severity grading is the weak link, at 0.66. The design pairs the system with human verification rather than replacing it." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Health, science & medicine",
      "headline": "Preprint: 15 LLMs scored 53% to 96% detecting inserted trial errors against 27% for human reviewers",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "The second one, posted on medRxiv, is about peer review itself. In an earlier experiment, errors were inserted into three reports of randomised clinical trials, and untrained human reviewers caught a mean of 27% of the major ones." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And the models?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Across 15 large language models, including four open-weight ones, major-error detection scores ranged from 53% to 96%. Mean review times ranged from 1.4 to 16.3 minutes." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "To reduce the risk of the models having memorised the papers, the authors repeated it on 58 trials published in 2026, and say the main findings held. One model found 94% of the inserted major errors." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The caveats matter. This is a preprint, not peer reviewed, and a single source. The 27% human figure comes from a separate earlier study with untrained reviewers, so it isn't head-to-head. The models were judged by other models. And the authors report false positives on control reports that had no inserted errors." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "Policy next, and a law that now has a date on it." }
      ]
    },
    {
